The code statements that lead to the bug are:
case I2C_SLAVE_READ_REQUESTED:
    midev->rx_pos = 0;
    break;
/* No case for I2C_SLAVE_READ_PROCESSED, so it falls through to default */
Reason:
These code statements do not set *val for I2C slave read events, causing the I2C bus driver to return stack-uninitialized data when a read is performed. Specifically, for I2C_SLAVE_READ_REQUESTED, *val is left uninitialized, and for I2C_SLAVE_READ_PROCESSED, the event is not handled at all, so *val remains uninitialized.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
case I2C_SLAVE_READ_REQUESTED:
case I2C_SLAVE_READ_PROCESSED:
    /* MCTP I2C transport only uses writes */
    midev->rx_pos = 0;
    *val = 0xff;
    break;
Reason:
These statements initialize *val to 0xff for both read-requested and read-processed events, ensuring that the I2C master receives a deterministic value (0xff) instead of uninitialized stack data. This aligns with the MCTP I2C transport only using writes, so reads should return 0xff.

